Blizzard PPC 68060 backup disk
=============================

Write this ADF to a floppy (or use a writable ADF in a floppy emulator).
Leave it write-enabled. Power on with S held to disable the Blizzard SCSI
driver, and boot this floppy in DF0:. Let its startup-sequence run.
This disk is for the 68060 Blizzard PPC, not the 68040 model.

It automatically runs:
  SYS:C/bppcid >SYS:backup.log --backup SYS:bppc-backup.bin
and, only after a successful backup:
  SYS:C/ncr7xx >SYS:probe.log -a bppc -d -R

bppc-backup.bin contains 393216 bytes (384 KiB) from 00f00000..00f5ffff,
including the factory header and configuration. Board I/O occupies the
next 128 KiB; this is not a full 512 KiB programmer image.

backup.log records the board checks, verification and CRC32. The saved
file is closed, reopened and compared byte for byte with the ROM snapshot.
probe.log records the board state and SCSI reset release/readback/restore
test. A rejected release request is a diagnostic result, not a backup error.
The probe refuses to run with the SCSI driver loaded. It does not access
the NCR registers or start disk I/O.

No flash write or ID change is requested. The reset probe temporarily
requests release of SCSI reset, then restores its original state.
No A4091 drivers, firmware, updater or support files are included.

An existing bppc-backup.bin prevents both commands from running again,
preserving the backup and its logs. This also preserves partial files after
a failed or interrupted backup: check backup.log before trusting the file.
Use a fresh copy of the ADF to retry. Wait for the floppy light to go out
before removing the disk, then copy the backup and logs to another machine.
